{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310781,"nonce":"5uFXJbBCRu1Yan/HN/yD1l+Gnfw=","title":"Cold start is tc.imports (255 of 294ms) because stdlib cache entries are keyed per-program; 29 of the 42-module closure declare no generics","message":"**MEASURED 2026-08-17 at `9875ac20`** on a `Sys.println` hello program.\n`RAYZOR_PROFILE_TYPECHECK=1` (also `RAYZOR_PROFILE_COMPILE=1`) prints a full\nphase breakdown — USE IT, do not guess or reach for a sampling profiler\nfirst:\n\n    typecheck-detail: files=52 imports=42 cache_hit=0 cache_miss=51 fresh=42\n    stdlib 21ms → parse 0.05ms → typecheck 267ms → tc.imports 255ms →\n      tc.user 10ms → tc.ast 68ms → tc.mir 27ms → tc.merge 7.3ms →\n      compile 294ms → shake 0.59ms → optimize 3.1ms → jit 0.02ms\n\n**tc.imports = 255ms of 294ms. cache_hit = 0.** In-process the FIRST compile\nis 238ms and every later one 12.5ms (`examples/stdlib_setup_profile`), so\nthis is one-time-per-process work, not setup: `new()` + `load_stdlib` are\n~7ms combined.\n\n**THE BREAKDOWN (instrumented `52308bdd`, all behind `profile_typecheck`):**\n\n    tc.imports              252ms\n    ├─ imp-discover          11ms   parse every import just to find its deps\n    ├─ imp-toposort         0.3ms\n    └─ imp-compile          240ms\n       ├─ cache-load         7.2ms\n       ├─ cache-save          14ms  writing 51 .blade files after the misses\n       ├─ compile-call       170ms  the 42 modules themselves\n       │   ├─ tc.ast          75ms  AST lowering / typecheck — biggest single item\n       │   ├─ tc.mir          22ms\n       │   ├─ tc.hir         6.4ms\n       │   └─ ~60ms unattributed inside compile_file_with_shared_state_ex\n       └─ ~49ms              bookkeeping in try_compile_import around the compile\n\nParsing is NOT the cost (discovery 11ms + file-parse 3.7ms). Cache I/O is\nNOT the cost (21ms). **Compiling the modules is**, and typechecking them is\nthe largest named part of it. Any plan that only avoids re-PARSING the\nlibrary is chasing 15ms of 252.\n\n**WHY zero hits — TWO checks fail, not one.** `RAYZOR_DEBUG_BLADE=1` prints\nboth (added `58f5de7f`; the pre-existing `trace!` is unreachable without a\nlogger and would not show values anyway):\n\n    [blade] .../haxe-std/Class.hx\n            hash  12291907578443100016 vs 6324098376373329426\n            build 52eb616ac3e199b6     vs 3bce270f3a9eb0ae\n\n1. **BUILD ID — was silently stale; now WARNS (`43d0d890`).**\n   `RAYZOR_BUILD_ID` (compiler/build.rs `cache_build_id`) hashes\n   `compiler/src/**` + haxe-std + parser, so the archive goes stale on any\n   compiler source change. It cannot be regenerated from build.rs — the\n   generator LINKS the compiler crate (and a build script that tried collided\n   across host/target graphs, see\n   [[project_stdlib_snapshot_embed_via_cli_buildrs]]). So `snapshot-gen` now\n   writes `target/stdlib_snapshot.build-id` and build.rs compares it, warning\n   when they differ. **Converging is: build → `cargo run --release -p\n   snapshot-gen` → build again.** Verified: warning fires, two-step clears it,\n   and all 51 entries then match on build id.\n\n2. **SOURCE HASH — the program fold.** After regenerating, the build ids match\n   and ONLY the source hash differs. For a stdlib module every other term\n   (source bytes, defines, import-set hash) is identical between generator and\n   consumer, so `user_program_hash()` is the single remaining blocker.\n\nThe snapshot IS wired in: `try_load_blade_cached_full` (compilation.rs ~4245)\nfalls back to `embedded_snapshot_entry` when the project has no `.blade`. The\narchive holds 126 entries, 42 per tier, keyed `\u003cdiscriminator\u003e/\u003cmodule\u003e.blade`\nfor all three of jit/llvm/interp — and the CLI's discriminator IS among them\n(do not conclude otherwise from the first few keys; they are a different tier).\n\n**THE ONE REMAINING BLOCKER: the program fold.** With the snapshot fresh, all\n51 entries match on build id and ONLY `source_hash` differs. For a stdlib\nmodule every other term of `hash_source_for_config` (source bytes, defines,\nimport-set hash) is identical between generator and consumer, so\n`user_program_hash()` is the whole of it.\n\n**TWO WAYS ALREADY PROVEN WRONG — do not retry either:**\n- dropping the fold entirely: `7997eedd`, reverted `e4ec2b11`\n- dropping it for modules that declare no generic:\n  [[bugs-nongeneric-stdlib-modules-still-program-dependent]]\n\n**THE DESIGN THAT COULD WORK (untried).** The dependency is not \"the program\"\nbut \"the generic instantiations this module was lowered against\". Record that\nset IN the cache entry, and on load accept it when the current program demands\nnothing outside it. That is what \"sharing can return when the restore path is\nproven equivalent\" (the comment at the fold) actually asks for, and it degrades\nsafely: an entry lowered for a program that instantiated nothing is valid only\nfor programs that instantiate nothing of that module.\n\n**ZYNTAX IS NOT A SOURCE TO COPY HERE — CHECKED, 2026-08-17.** Their\n`typed_ast/src/ast_convert.rs` `type_cache` has exactly one caller: a unit\ntest. `multi_paradigm_checker.rs` `type_cache` is declared, initialised empty,\nand never read or written. `hir_builder.rs` `TypeCache` is real but is twelve\npreallocated primitive `HirType`s, not a typecheck-result cache. Only\n`typed_ast/src/import_resolver.rs` (caching `ExportedSymbol`/`ResolvedImport`)\nis wired. READ THEIR CODE BEFORE PLANNING ON IT — twice this session a\ndescribed mechanism turned out not to exist.\n\n**AND A MEMO TABLE WOULD NOT HELP ANYWAY:** the 75ms of `tc.ast` is\ntypechecking 42 module BODIES, not repeated type resolution. The only ways out\nare to reuse a prior result (the snapshot) or not reach the modules.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_cold_start_is_tc_imports` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}